    "body": {
      "type": "pm_learn_body",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_2t": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The Confusion Is Understandable",
            "toc_label": "The confusion is fair"
          }
        },
        "text_2u": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Walk into any health food store and you'll find shelves of products with “cacao” on the label. Cacao powder. Raw cacao nibs. Cacao butter. Cacao paste. Ceremonial cacao. Dark chocolate with 85% cacao. They all come from the same bean. They all look related. Some are even marketed with nearly identical health claims.</p><p>So what's actually different? Is ceremonial cacao just expensive cacao powder with a spiritual story attached to it? Or is there a genuine, meaningful distinction between them?</p><p>There is. And it matters more than most people realize, not just for the experience of drinking cacao, but for the actual health benefits you receive from it. What follows explains exactly what cacao powder is, how it's made, what gets lost in that process, and why whole-food ceremonial cacao is a fundamentally different product. Not a premium version of the same thing. A different thing entirely.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_2v": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Where They Both Begin, The Cacao Bean",
            "toc_label": "Where they both begin"
          }
        },
        "text_2w": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Both cacao powder and ceremonial cacao start from the same place: the seeds of the Theobroma cacao tree, harvested from pods, fermented, and dried. At this point, a cacao bean is roughly half fat and half solids.</p>"
          }
        },
        "compare_2x": {
          "type": "compare",
          "settings": {
            "a_label": "",
            "a_value": "",
            "a_text": "",
            "b_label": "",
            "b_value": "",
            "b_text": ""
          }
        },
        "text_2y": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Cacao butter is the rich, stable natural fat. Cacao solids are the fibrous, mineral-rich, flavonoid-dense dry matter.</p><p>This 50/50 composition is not incidental. It is how nature designed the cacao bean, and it is the whole-food form in which cacao has been consumed, as medicine and as food, for thousands of years. Everything that happens after this point, the processing decisions made by whoever turns that bean into a finished product, determines what you actually receive when you drink it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_2z": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What Happens To Cacao Powder",
            "toc_label": "What happens to powder"
          }
        },
        "text_30": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Cacao powder is made through an industrial process called pressing. Here is what that looks like, step by step.</p><ol><li> 1 High-heat roasting<p>Beans are roasted at high temperatures to force uniformity across large batches of inconsistent, commodity-grade beans. This degrades flavonoids and polyphenols before any other step begins.</p></li><li> 2 Grinding into paste<p>The roasted beans become cacao liquor, a smooth liquid that is essentially 100% whole cacao. At this stage the product is actually nutritionally complete. The problem comes next.</p></li><li> 3 Hydraulic pressing<p>Enormous pressure separates the liquor into cacao butter (the fat, pressed out) and a dry, dense cake of cacao solids (what remains after the fat is gone).</p></li><li> 4 Pulverizing into powder<p>That dry cake is ground into the fine particles you know as cacao powder.</p></li></ol>"
          }
        },
        "callout_31": {
          "type": "callout",
          "settings": {
            "label": "",
            "text": "<p>The critical thing to understand: this is done primarily to obtain the cacao butter, not the powder. Cacao butter is a high-value ingredient for luxury cosmetics, pharmaceuticals, and premium confectionery. The powder is the byproduct, what remains after the valuable fat has been extracted, and it is then marketed as a superfood.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_32": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Some powders undergo an additional step called Dutch processing, or alkalization, treatment with an alkaline solution to reduce acidity, darken the color, and mellow the flavor. This further reduces polyphenol content, sometimes dramatically. If you see “processed with alkali” or “Dutch process” on a label, the flavonoid content is likely far lower than the raw number suggests.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_33": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What Gets Lost When The Fat Is Removed",
            "toc_label": "What gets lost"
          }
        },
        "text_34": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The removal of cacao butter is not a neutral step. It fundamentally changes what cacao is and what it can do in your body.</p>"
          }
        },
        "cards_35": {
          "type": "cards",
          "settings": {
            "columns": 1,
            "label_1": "The Carrier",
            "text_1": "<p>Many of cacao's beneficial compounds are not water-soluble and require fat to be absorbed. Cacao butter is the specific fat nature designed to carry them, and it does the job better than any fat you add back in later.</p>",
            "label_2": "Mineral Balance",
            "text_2": "<p>Minerals live in both the fat and solids of the bean. Remove the fat and the ratios shift. You get a concentrated but incomplete nutritional picture.</p>",
            "label_3": "Energetic Quality",
            "text_3": "<p>Harder to quantify but consistently reported: whole-food cacao has an aliveness and presence that powder lacks, a reflection of intact biochemistry working together as a system.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_36": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>When you remove the cacao butter and then try to compensate by mixing powder with coconut oil, you are not recreating whole-food cacao. You are attempting to reconstruct something that was never meant to be disassembled, and the body can tell the difference.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_37": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What Ceremonial Cacao Is Instead",
            "toc_label": "What ceremonial cacao is"
          }
        },
        "text_38": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Ceremonial cacao is whole-food cacao. Nothing added, nothing removed. The whole bean, after careful fermentation, sun-drying, and low-temperature roasting, is ground into a smooth paste that keeps the full 50/50 ratio of butter to solids. Every beneficial compound is present. Every mineral is in its natural proportion. The fat and the solids are together, as they were in the bean.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image_39": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "alt": "Photo: a block of whole-food Chuncho cacao paste",
            "image_url": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0725/1188/1261/files/learn-img-155c1ec79f.webp?v=1785782948"
          }
        },
        "text_3a": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>This is not a modern innovation. It is the oldest form of prepared cacao that exists. Ground stone, open fire, whole bean: this is how cacao has been made for thousands of years across Mesoamerica and the Andes. Ceremonial cacao is not a new product category. It is the original one.</p><p>What makes Pacha Mana's ceremonial cacao worth understanding is the starting point: Chuncho, an ancient heirloom variety grown at high elevation in the Sacred Valley of Peru. Chuncho is not a high-yield commercial hybrid. It produces smaller pods, yields less per tree, and requires more careful attention at every stage. Smallholder family farmers tend it by hand.</p><p>When the starting material is this good, grown in clean high-altitude soil, fermented with care, harvested by people who understand what they're handling, you don't need to correct it downstream. You don't need high heat to mask off-flavors. You don't need alkalization to smooth out bitterness. You need only to handle it with respect, preserve what's there, and get it into your cup intact.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_3b": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Side By Side, The Real Comparison",
            "toc_label": "Side by side"
          }
        },
        "compare_3c": {
          "type": "compare",
          "settings": {
            "a_label": "",
            "a_value": "",
            "a_text": "",
            "b_label": "",
            "b_value": "",
