          }
        },
        "heading_3d": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "\"But I've Used Cacao Powder For Years And Feel Fine\"",
            "toc_label": "\"But I feel fine\""
          }
        },
        "text_3e": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>This is a fair point, and we want to address it honestly. Cacao powder is not harmful. It still contains meaningful amounts of magnesium, iron, and some flavonoids, less than whole-food cacao, but not zero. If you've been using it in smoothies or baking and enjoying the benefits, that's real.</p><p>The question is not whether cacao powder has any value. The question is whether it delivers the full experience and full spectrum of benefits that whole-food ceremonial cacao does. The answer to that is clearly no.</p><p>Think of it like comparing whole milk to skim milk. Skim milk still has protein and calcium. It's not nothing. But it's missing the fat-soluble vitamins, the fat itself, and the integrated nutritional profile of the whole food. No matter how many fortifications you add back in, it isn't the same thing as what came out of the animal.</p>"
          }
        },
        "pullquote_3f": {
          "type": "pullquote",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Cacao powder is cacao that has been taken apart. Ceremonial cacao is cacao that has been left alone.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_3g": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What About Raw Cacao Powder?",
            "toc_label": "Raw cacao powder?"
          }
        },
        "text_3h": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>You may have seen cacao powder labeled “raw” and assumed it means less processed or more nutritionally intact than regular cocoa powder. In most cases, this is primarily a marketing distinction. Raw cacao powder has still had its cacao butter pressed out. It is still an industrial byproduct of butter extraction, just potentially processed at lower temperatures. The fat is still gone. The fundamental limitation of cacao powder remains.</p><p>For a full explanation of why “raw cacao” is largely a myth, and what actually preserves the medicine in cacao, see our guide: <a href=\"/pages/learn-raw-cacao-myth\">Raw Cacao Is a Myth, And Here's What We Do Instead</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_3i": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What About Adding Cacao Butter Back?",
            "toc_label": "Adding butter back?"
          }
        },
        "text_3j": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Some people, having learned about the importance of cacao butter, try to reconstruct whole cacao by combining powder with separately purchased cacao butter. The logic seems reasonable. The result is not quite what they're hoping for.</p>"
          }
        },
        "cards_3k": {
          "type": "cards",
          "settings": {
            "columns": 1,
            "label_1": "",
            "text_1": "<p><strong>The fat was already separated under pressure and heat.</strong> Pressing changes both the powder and the butter. Recombining them is not the same as never separating them.</p>",
            "label_2": "",
            "text_2": "<p><strong>The ratios are hard to get right.</strong> The precise 50/50 balance of a whole bean is nearly impossible to recreate by eye at home.</p>",
            "label_3": "",
            "text_3": "<p><strong>The sourcing is usually separate and low quality.</strong> You are combining two commodity byproducts and hoping for a medicinal whole food.</p>",
            "label_4": "",
            "text_4": "<p><strong>It costs more and is more complicated</strong> than simply starting with whole-food cacao in the first place.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_3l": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>If the goal is the full benefit of cacao, start with cacao that was never taken apart.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_3m": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How To Use Ceremonial Cacao",
            "toc_label": "How to use it"
          }
        },
        "text_3n": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Ceremonial cacao paste or ground cacao is straightforward to prepare, and produces a drink that is richer, more satisfying, and more medicinally complete than anything made from powder.</p><ol><li> 1 <p>Warm 6 to 8 oz of spring water or plant milk to just below boiling, steamy, not rolling.</p></li><li> 2 <p>Add 1 to 2 tablespoons of Pacha Mana Chuncho cacao paste or ground cacao to your mug.</p></li><li> 3 <p>Add a small splash of hot liquid and whisk vigorously until fully dissolved.</p></li><li> 4 <p>Pour in the remaining liquid slowly, continuing to whisk or using a handheld frother.</p></li><li> 5 <p>Add a pinch of sea salt. Optional: cinnamon, cayenne, or a little maple syrup or honey. Then pause before the first sip and set an intention.</p></li></ol><p>The texture is different from a powder-based drink: richer, more velvety, with a body that comes from the cacao butter naturally emulsified into the liquid. This is what chocolate was always meant to taste like.</p>"
          }
        },
        "heading_3o": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The Bottom Line"
          }
        },
        "text_3p": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Cacao powder and ceremonial cacao share a name and an origin. Beyond that, they are different products with different nutritional profiles, different preparation histories, and different relationships to the plant they came from.</p><p>Cacao powder is a useful ingredient. It belongs in brownies and smoothies and the back of your pantry. Ceremonial cacao is medicine. It belongs in your morning ritual, in your hands, warm, made with intention, from a bean that someone grew carefully and a farmer harvested by hand.</p><p>The difference is not just what's in the cup. It's what you receive from it.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
